# React + TypeScript Conventions

## Component Structure
- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces for props
- Export components as default exports
- Max 150 lines per component — split if larger
- One component per file, named same as file (PascalCase)

## TypeScript
- Enable strict mode: `"strict": true` in tsconfig.json
- Always type function parameters and return values
- Use interfaces over types for object shapes (extensibility)
- Use `unknown` over `any` — never use `any`
- Prefer `const` assertions and discriminated unions

## Naming
- Components: PascalCase (`ExpenseForm.tsx`)
- Hooks: camelCase with `use` prefix (`useExpenses.ts`)
- Utils: camelCase (`formatCurrency.ts`)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase with `I` prefix only if needed
- Boolean vars: `is/has/should` prefix (`isLoading`, `hasError`)
- Event handlers: `handle` prefix (`handleSubmit`, `handleClick`)

## State Management
- Dexie.js + dexie-react-hooks for persistent data (useLiveQuery)
- Zustand for UI-only ephemeral state
- Never mix: persistent data in Dexie, UI state in Zustand
- Avoid prop drilling > 2 levels — use Zustand or context

## Performance
- Memoize expensive computations with `useMemo`
- Memoize callbacks with `useCallback` when passed as props
- Use `React.lazy()` for route-level code splitting
- Avoid inline object/array creation in JSX props

## Error Handling
- Wrap async operations in try/catch
- Show user-friendly error messages in Italian
- Log technical errors to console in English
- Use Error Boundaries for component-level error catching
